  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Luo\"\nsubtitle: \"Kenya\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | A Western Nilotic people of the eastern Lake Victoria basin |\n| Where | Western Kenya and northwestern Tanzania, between 3,700 and 6,000 feet above sea level |\n| How many | 5,066,966 people (2019 Kenyan census) |\n| Language | Dholuo, of the Western Nilotic branch |\n| Religion | Christian with nativist strands |\n| Known for | originators of benga and ohangla music · stone-walled fortresses · tooth-removal puberty rite · mixed economy of cattle, farming and fishing |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Luo are Kenya's fourth-largest ethnic group, living around Lake Victoria. Their traditional economy combined cattle pastoralism, seed farming and fishing, supplemented by hunting. They speak Dholuo and are credited as originators of the popular music genres benga and ohangla. The surviving material record shows pots, hoes, fish-country basketry, cattle gear and horn and ivory ornament.\n\n## Material culture\n\nLuo material culture shows pattern work in looped and knotted string, plaited leather, glass beads, cowrie shell, and impressed surface work on clay, wood and hide.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nPattern is carried by looped and knotted string, plaited leather, glass beads, cowrie shell, and impressed or incised surface work rather than loom-woven cloth.\n\n- **String garment** — A black garment of cords looped and knotted onto a string base and finished with a fringe, worn by married women.\n- **Plaited-leather waist apron** — A plaited leather apron closed with a fringe of red, white and dark blue glass beads.\n- **Cowrie-shell surface work** — Cowries are applied as a dense covering on leather head-dresses, conical pale-hide caps, leather head ornaments and arm-dagger sheaths.\n- **Bead-inlaid woodwork** — A large circular four-legged wooden stool is inlaid with glass beads set into geometric decoration.\n- **Rouletted and cross-hatched earthenware** — A globular two-lugged pot bears rouletted ornament and a tobacco-pipe bowl carries punched bands, raised vertical bands and incised cross-hatching.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nEveryday cloth and necklaces contrast sharply with ceremonial and status headgear, which is the richest part of the collection.\n\n- **Black knotted string garment** — Worn by a married woman, with a looped fringe.\n- **Plaited-leather waist apron** — Features red, white and dark blue bead fringe.\n- **Basketwork hat** — Plaited grass with exterior plastered with clay and set with plumes of cock's feathers.\n- **Conical pale-hide cap** — Covered with cowrie shell and trimmed with white feathers and down, with two long ear-pendants covered entirely with cowrie shell.\n- **Zebra-mane head-dress** — A composite head-dress of leather with a zebra-mane crest, conus and cowrie ornament, and buttons.\n\n### Architecture\n\nLuo building is a defensive tradition of the savanna, with stone-walled enclosures built in response to conflict and raiding in the crowded Lake Victoria basin.\n\n- **Thimlich Ohinga** (*ohingni*) — The best-known stone-walled fortress in South Nyanza, with most of the stone walling dating to the period of Luo expansion.\n- **Defensive earthworks** (*gunda bur*) — Earthworks curving around the living area they enclosed, found in both northern and southern Nyanza.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nPottery is the best-documented Luo craft and women's work, with hand-built earthenware bowls and pots supplemented by iron tools and wooden household items.\n\n- **Earthenware pots** — Hand-built globular two-lugged pots with rouletted ornament, and bowls with flat bases.\n- **Iron hoe** — With separate iron blade and wooden handle.\n- **Arm-dagger** — Iron and wood with a cowrie-and-tassel sheath.\n- **Wooden stool** — A large circular four-legged stool inlaid with glass beads in geometric decoration.\n- **Carved wooden cowbell** — Hung on a fibre cord for herding livestock.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nOrnament is worked from animal material — ivory, tusk, horn, shell and snake bone — rather than metal.\n\n- **Ivory armlets** — Made of solid ivory or from two boar tusks bound together with leather thongs.\n- **Hippopotamus tusk forehead ornament** — A crescentic ornament cut from a section of hippopotamus tusk and pierced twice at the centre.\n- **Composite neck ornament** — Combines tusk and wood with a leather cap attached to a necklace strung from snake vertebrae.\n- **Cowrie-covered head ornament** — Filled and covered with cowries, fitted with two small curved antelope horns and recorded as warrior's wear.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nThe Luo originated benga and ohangla music genres and built a chordophone-and-drum ensemble from imported manufactures and local materials.\n\n- **Five-string lyre** — Has its resonator made from a green enamel bowl closed with a hide membrane, with strings passing over a bridge improvised from a blackened cotton reel.\n- **Bamboo zither** — Finished with vegetable fibre and dung.\n- **Musical bow** — Wood with a gourd resonator.\n- **Composite drum** — A wooden body covered at both ends with brown-haired skin, the two heads lashed together.\n- **Musical pipe** — Cut from a stalk of maize, an instrument made from the harvest itself.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nThe sources record no Luo dance or dramatic tradition.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nComing-of-age rite and ritual complexes centered on the removal of the six lower teeth at puberty, which named the people and became an instrument of cultural incorporation.\n\n- **Tooth-removal rite** (*Jonagi / Onagi*) — Removal of the six lower teeth at puberty as a definitive puberty rite, making those who undergo it 'one whose lower teeth have been removed'.\n- **Mumboism** (*Mumbo*) — A religious movement begun in 1913 by Onyango Dunde, pledging to drive out colonisers and condemning their religion, spreading among Luo and Kisii before suppression through the 1920s and 1930s.\n\n## Foodways\n\nThe traditional economy combined cattle pastoralism, seed cultivation and fishing, supplemented by hunting, a combination visible as far back as the Kadero site dated to 3000 BC.\n\n- **Wooden pestle** — For pounding food.\n- **Earthenware pots and flat-based bowls** — For cooking and serving.\n- **Basketwork dish** — Lined with cow dung to make it hold its contents.\n- **Grass decoy quail trap** — For catching small game.\n- **Iron-bladed hoe** — For cultivating the seed fields.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nLuo oral history is precise enough to have been used as chronology, dating four waves of migration into Nyanza and encoding descent through group names and the prefix Jo-, 'people of'.\n\n- **People of Jok** (*Joka Jok*) — The first and largest migration wave, beginning around 1490–1517 and settling at Ramogi Hill.\n- **Ramogi's heirs** (*Joramogi / Nyikwaramogi*) — A descent group name encoding the connection to the first migration wave and Ramogi Hill.\n- **Dholuo print culture** — Arthur Carscallen, a Canadian Seventh-day Adventist, first reduced Dholuo to writing and produced a grammar textbook and Bible translation, followed by vernacular publications like Achieng Oneko's newspaper Ramogi.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nDholuo belongs to the Western Nilotic branch, with measured lexical similarity to neighbouring Luo languages tracing the migration route, while the religious landscape is Christian with a nativist strand.\n\n- **Dholuo language** — Western Nilotic branch of the Nilotic family within Nilo-Saharan, with 93% lexical similarity to Dhopadhola, 90% to Lep Alur, 83% to Lep Achol, 74% to Anuak, and 69% with Jurchol and Dhi-Pari.\n- **Luo script** — A separate Luo script was invented around 2021 to write the Luo languages.\n- **Seventh-day Adventist mission** — Arthur Carscallen and Peter Nyambo opened the first mission in November 1906 at Gendia Hill, Kendu Bay, with help from German missionary Abraham C. Enns, and baptised the first Luo Adventist converts on 21 May 1911.\n- **Mumboism** (*Mumbo*) — A nativist religious movement in the 1910s–1930s centred on the serpent goddess of Lake Victoria, supplying an explicitly anti-colonial cosmology.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Dholuo* — The Western Nilotic language spoken by the Luo\n- *benga* — A popular African music genre originated by the Luo\n- *ohangla* — A music genre originated by the Luo\n- *ohingni* — Stone-walled fortresses built by the Luo\n- *gunda bur* — Defensive earthworks curving around enclosed living areas\n- *Jonagi* — A person whose six lower teeth have been removed as a puberty rite\n- *Onagi* — Singular form of Jonagi\n- *Jo-* — Prefix meaning 'people of', used to build names of Luo tribes, clans and sub-clans\n- *Joka Jok* — The People of Jok, the first and largest migration wave into Nyanza\n- *Joramogi* — Ramogi's heirs, a descent group name\n- *Nyikwaramogi* — An alternative name for Ramogi's heirs\n- *Mumbo* — The serpent goddess of Lake Victoria in nativist Luo religion\n- *Mumboism* — A nativist religious movement centred on the serpent goddess Mumbo\n- *Thimlich Ohinga* — The best-known stone-walled fortress of the Luo in South Nyanza\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- Wikipedia, \"Luo people\" — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luo_people\n- Wikipedia, \"Luo language\" — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luo_language\n- British Museum online collection — https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection\n- Neville Chittick, on the dating of Thimlich Ohinga — cited in the Wikipedia article above for the argument that the stone walling predates Luo settlement\n- Smithsonian Folkways, search for Luo and Kenyan recordings — https://folkways.si.edu/search?query=Luo+Kenya\n- UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage, Kenya — https://ich.unesco.org/en/state/kenya\n\n",
